# Calling a builtin that does not exist must be an ERROR, not a value.
#
# L_CALL_BUILTIN looked the name up and, when the lookup failed, fell straight
# through to the `r1 <- top-of-stack` pop at the end of the case -- so the call
# returned whatever had been pushed last.  `str2int("42")` (registered only by
# io_mod_register, which is a no-op stub on POSIX) handed back the string "42"
# itself: `say str(str2int("42"))` printed 42 while `str2int("42") == 42` was
# false, and the program carried on with that value.
#
# The interpreter was the lone outlier.  Both compilation backends already
# refuse the same program at compile time --
#   aot_native: call to 'nosuchbuiltin' is not a user-defined function in this program
#   error: wasm MVP subset: function 'nosuchbuiltin' not found (builtins are not in the wasm MVP subset)
# -- so this makes the third backend agree with the other two instead of
# inventing a value.  The error is an ordinary throw: it is catchable, and the
# program continues normally afterwards.
#
# The name below is deliberately one that exists in NO build, so the test does
# not depend on which modules a platform happens to link.
#
# NOTE for anyone editing the regexes in CMakeLists.txt: the engine also prints
# a dump of its STRING POOL on stderr, so a FAIL_REGULAR_EXPRESSION that quotes a
# literal appearing in this file matches that dump too, and the test reports a
# false failure.  That is why the states below are integers and the printed keys
# are built by concatenation: `state=0`, `state=2` and `control=false` occur
# nowhere in the pool.  (The first version of this test failed for exactly that
# reason, with the implementation already correct.)
#
# See docs/AUDIT.md §1.11.
state = 0
try {
    junk = definitely_not_a_builtin_xyz(1)
} catch (e) {
    if str(e) == "unknown builtin function 'definitely_not_a_builtin_xyz'" { state = 1 } else { state = 2 }
}
say "unreg-ok state=" + str(state) + " control=" + str(str(42) == "42")
